Step 1 — Install Jenkins on this same EC2 instance
sudo wget -O /etc/yum.repos.d/jenkins.repo https://pkg.jenkins.io/rpm-stable/jenkins.repo
yum upgrade -y
yum install -y fontconfig java-21-amazon-corretto
yum install jenkins -y
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable jenkins
systemctl start jenkins
systemctl status jenkins

systemctl enable is what makes it "start automatically at boot" as the task requires.

Step 2 — Open port 8080 for Jenkins
Since Tomcat moved off 8080 in Question 5, this port is free — but still need it explicitly allowed inbound if it isn't already: EC2 → Security Groups → SG → Inbound rules → Add rule → Custom TCP, port 8080, source Anywhere.

Step 3 — First login
cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword

Browser: http://<EC2-Public-IP>:8080 → paste that password → skip "Install suggested plugins" for now (close the dialog) → create admin user when prompted.

Install just what is needed: Manage Jenkins → Plugins → Available plugins → search and install:
Git plugin (usually bundled already — check Installed tab first)
Maven Integration plugin
GitHub plugin

Step 4 — Global Tool Configuration
Manage Jenkins → Tools:

JDK:
Name: jdk21
JAVA_HOME: /usr/lib/jvm/java-21-amazon-corretto.x86_64

Uncheck "Install automatically" if it's ticked — you already installed Java in Question 4.

Maven:
Name: maven3
MAVEN_HOME: /opt/maven

Same — uncheck "Install automatically," you already have it at /opt/maven.
Save.

Step 5 — Get Jenkins authenticated to private repo

GitHub repo is private, so Jenkins needs its own credential — don't rely on /root/.ssh/id_rsa existing on disk, because the Jenkins service runs as its own jenkins OS user (not root) and can't read root's private key files even though you're logged into the terminal as root. The fix is to paste the key directly into Jenkins as a stored credential, which works regardless of which OS user the Jenkins process runs as.

Generate a dedicated key for Jenkins (cleaner than reusing personal one):

ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -m PEM -f /root/jenkins_key -N ""
cat /root/jenkins_key.pub

Copy that public key → GitHub → Settings → SSH and GPG keys → New SSH key → paste → Add.

cat /root/jenkins_key

Copy the entire output including the -----BEGIN and -----END lines.

In Jenkins: Manage Jenkins → Credentials → System → Global credentials → + Add Credentials

Kind: SSH Username with private key
ID: github-ssh-key
Username: git
Private Key: Enter directly → paste the full key
Passphrase: (leave empty)

Save.

Step 6 — Create the Jenkins job

New Item → name it devops-ci-s123456 (student ID) → Freestyle project → OK.

Source Code Management → Git:

Repository URL: git@github.com:rmit-vietnam-computing-technologies/2026b-cosc2767-a1-<repo-name>.git
Credentials: github-ssh-key (select from dropdown)
Branches to build: */develop

Build → Add build step → Invoke top-level Maven targets:

Maven Version: maven3
Goals: clean package
POM: question-4/my_project/pom.xml

The POM path matters here — Jenkins clones whole repo into its workspace, so it needs the path relative to the repo root to find actual Maven project buried inside question-4/.

Save the job, click Build Now, and confirm it succeeds:

Jenkins job page → Build History → click the build number → Console Output → should end in "BUILD SUCCESS"

Step 7 — Set up the GitHub webhook

GitHub repo → Settings → Webhooks → Add webhook:

Payload URL: http://<EC2-Public-IP>:8080/github-webhook/
Content type: application/json
Secret: (leave blank)
Which events: Just the push event
Active: checked

Add webhook.

Back in the Jenkins job config → Build Triggers → check "GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling" → Save.

Step 8 — Prove the automated trigger works
cd ~/2026b-cosc2767-a1-<repo-name>
git checkout develop
mkdir -p question-6
echo "Testing automated Jenkins build trigger via GitHub webhook - $(date)" > question-6/build-trigger.txt
git add question-6/build-trigger.txt
git commit -m "Test automated Jenkins build trigger"
git push

Within a few seconds, check Jenkins Build History — a new build should appear without you clicking Build Now. Click into it → Console Output should show near the top something like Started by GitHub push by <username> (rather than "Started by user admin").

Step 9 — Commit configuration files
cp /root/jenkins_key.pub question-6/
cd ~/2026b-cosc2767-a1-<repo-name>
git add question-6/
git status
git commit -m "Add Jenkins job configuration evidence for Question 6"
git push
